### Article Summary

Computer architecture refers to the structure, design, and organization of a computer system and explains how its hardware components interact to execute instructions and process information. It provides a connection between hardware and software and has a major influence on a computer’s performance, efficiency, and overall functionality. With the rapid development of technology, modern computer architecture aims to achieve higher processing speeds, better energy efficiency, and improved support for applications such as Artificial Intelligence (AI), cloud computing, and the Internet of Things (IoT).

A computer system is made up of several essential components, including the Central Processing Unit (CPU), memory, storage, input/output devices, and buses. The CPU acts as the main processing unit and executes instructions using the fetch-decode-execute cycle. Memory temporarily stores programs and data required during processing, while storage provides long-term data retention. These components communicate with one another through buses, which include data, address, and control buses.

One of the fundamental concepts in computer architecture is the **Von Neumann Architecture**. In this model, both program instructions and data are stored in the same memory, making the system simpler and easier to organize. Another important concept is the distinction between **RISC (Reduced Instruction Set Computer)** and **CISC (Complex Instruction Set Computer)** architectures. RISC uses a smaller number of simple instructions that can generally be executed quickly, whereas CISC provides a larger set of more complex instructions that can perform multiple operations.

Advancements such as multi-core processors, pipelining, cache memory, parallel processing, GPUs, and AI accelerators have significantly improved computing performance. These technologies enable faster processing, efficient multitasking, and support for demanding applications. Computer architecture is also widely used in smartphones, embedded systems, robotics, industrial automation, and other modern technologies.

In conclusion, computer architecture is the fundamental framework of a computing system. A strong understanding of its concepts enables engineers to develop computers that are faster, more efficient, reliable, and capable of handling increasingly complex applications. As technology continues to advance, computer architecture will remain an essential area in the development of future computing systems.
